  "text": "Question six. How does a merge support idempotent upserts? It matches each staged row to the target on a key. A match updates, and no match inserts. Here Ben has a new name, and Dee is new. The first merge updates Ben and inserts Dee: four customers. Run the same batch again, and it matches both, writes the values they already hold, and inserts nothing: still four. A blind insert of that batch into the original three, twice, leaves seven rows. It's the statement a merge-style incremental strategy runs, keyed on the unique key, though which strategy you get depends on the adapter and on your config. What a merge can't do is delete. A row removed from the source never reaches the batch, so the target keeps it.",
